@Ιασων

So this is a legitimate controversy and has merit; and you may be more sensitive to it as a Greek native. (I mean we wouldn’t stand for any occupation called “slave” in English no matter the context). I certainly think it’s very insensitive to use it to mean any random assistant or worker. 

Still it’s hard to ignore a term that seems to be the preferred one for many if not most doulas (correct me if I’m wrong.) I might too; it would connect me with an ancient and respected profession regardless of the status of the practitioners. It wouldn’t be the first time a negative term was co-opted to a group to be a positive one (see “Queer” and “Yankee” although admittedly they don’t have the force of slavery, of course). Last, abhorrent as it was, slavery was an accepted and almost ubiquitous practice for thousands of years; it would be hard to erase its long literary and cultural ramifications. Nevertheless, a point duly noted.

@Ιασων

As a now-retired doula (both birth and postpartum) I sort of understand the criticism. At first, I had trouble embracing that job title, although it wasn't a big problem for me as in Montreal I went by "accompagnante à la naissance". However, over the years, the profession has gained enormous respect in the population, and even (most of the time) in hospitals. Now doula is widely used, even in French, and has a very positive connotation. I'm sure there are many words in the English language that have origins that do not fit the current usage. 

Not sure who the crowd having palpitations is.

